WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.069
This is Loadline:

2
00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.155
advanced planning and scheduling for make-to-order manufacturers.

3
00:00:05.155 --> 00:00:10.624
One connected system, from the sales plan to the shop floor, purchasing and the stores.

4
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:14.976
In most plants, planning still lives in spreadsheets.

5
00:00:14.976 --> 00:00:22.315
Planners re-key sales orders, check capacity by hand, chase buyers for parts, and walk the stores to count stock.

6
00:00:22.315 --> 00:00:27.576
That is hours of manual effort every day, and decisions made on yesterday's data.

7
00:00:28.476 --> 00:00:30.660
It starts with the marketing plan.

8
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:38.755
Upload the Excel file, and Loadline checks every row, flags duplicates and missing data, and keeps backups of earlier uploads.

9
00:00:38.755 --> 00:00:39.911
No more re-typing.

10
00:00:40.843 --> 00:00:49.852
Every sales order gets a start date and a finished-goods date automatically, scheduled backward from the customer date against real capacity.

11
00:00:49.852 --> 00:00:54.070
When the plan is right, one click sends it to ERP for the MRP run.

12
00:00:54.976 --> 00:01:01.349
Factory loading shows units in process per day against capacity, so overloads are visible weeks ahead.

13
00:01:01.349 --> 00:01:08.472
The factory calendar holds holidays and shutdowns, and the shop floor screen records breakdowns and actual output.

14
00:01:08.472 --> 00:01:11.659
Shortfalls carry over to the next day on their own.

15
00:01:12.576 --> 00:01:17.025
After MRP, purchasing sees the bill of materials of every work order:

16
00:01:17.025 --> 00:01:23.022
what stores, inspection and open purchase orders cover, and exactly what to buy, and by when.

17
00:01:23.022 --> 00:01:29.856
Each buyer sees their own material groups, and late purchase orders are flagged before they stop the line.

18
00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:34.319
The forecast tab learns from six months of plan history.

19
00:01:34.319 --> 00:01:42.544
It forecasts order volume, then sets safety stock and reorder points for every part, from demand variation and supplier lead time.

20
00:01:42.544 --> 00:01:45.518
Any shortfall flows straight into the buy list.

21
00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:54.836
In the warehouse, goods receipt, incoming inspection, stores by bin, work in progress, blocked stock and scrap are all live.

22
00:01:54.836 --> 00:02:01.287
Consumables are issued first-expiry, first-out, and expired stock moves to scrap automatically.

23
00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:05.915
Loadline fits any make-to-order or engineer-to-order plant:

24
00:02:05.915 --> 00:02:15.380
electric motors and generators, transformers and switchgear, pumps and compressors, heavy fabrication, automotive components, and industrial machinery.

25
00:02:16.284 --> 00:02:19.737
Loadline is measured the way plants measure themselves.

26
00:02:19.737 --> 00:02:20.553
Productivity:

27
00:02:20.553 --> 00:02:23.064
hours of planning work, done in minutes.

28
00:02:23.064 --> 00:02:23.566
Quality:

29
00:02:23.566 --> 00:02:28.149
one source of truth, no copy errors, and no expired material on the line.

30
00:02:28.149 --> 00:02:28.463
Cost:

31
00:02:28.463 --> 00:02:32.104
less manual effort, less expediting, and leaner inventory.

32
00:02:32.104 --> 00:02:32.669
Delivery:

33
00:02:32.669 --> 00:02:35.683
realistic dates, and early warning of shortages.

34
00:02:35.683 --> 00:02:36.122
Safety:

35
00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.257
fewer rush jobs and less overtime.

36
00:02:38.257 --> 00:02:38.696
Morale:

37
00:02:38.696 --> 00:02:41.270
people work on decisions, not data entry.

38
00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:42.024
Environment:

39
00:02:42.024 --> 00:02:43.719
less scrap, and less paper.

40
00:02:44.651 --> 00:02:48.275
Here is the cost case for a typical two-hundred-person plant.

41
00:02:48.275 --> 00:02:53.918
Planning, purchasing and stores spend about one hundred and twenty hours a week on manual work.

42
00:02:53.918 --> 00:02:56.592
Loadline removes around eighty percent of it:

43
00:02:56.592 --> 00:02:59.443
more than four and a half thousand hours a year.

44
00:02:59.443 --> 00:03:06.453
Add leaner inventory and half the expediting, and the saving is around four hundred and fifty thousand dollars a year.

45
00:03:07.354 --> 00:03:07.912
Loadline.

46
00:03:07.912 --> 00:03:09.400
Every load, on the line.

47
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.306
Plan with confidence, buy on time, and deliver on your promise.
